---
/**
 * About Axcess: why it exists, how it grew, what it holds itself to, where
 * it is going, and who builds it.
 *
 * Order is the story in time (past, present, future), then the people
 * behind it and where to find the source. A reader who wants only "who
 * builds this" finds it in the last section and in the "On this page" list.
 *
 * The history follows the project's white paper
 * (whitepaper/AXCESS-WHITE-PAPER.md, sections 2 and 3) and the primary
 * persona (docs/internal/personas.md). The direction cards follow the white
 * paper's section 5. Who builds Axcess follows README.md:17-18. The old page
 * named no individual author and did not link a personal site, so this page
 * does not either.
 *
 * Story sections are plain prose with Starlight's article styles. The
 * principles and direction are card grids, one idea per card (COGA "Making
 * Content Usable": one idea per chunk), using the shared Card so they look
 * like every other card on the site (SC 3.2.4 Consistent Identification,
 * Level AA).
 */
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import LinkButton from "../components/LinkButton.astro";
import coverage from "../data/coverage.json";
import { REPO, url } from "../lib/site";

const { covered, total } = coverage.summary;
const WHITEPAPER = `${REPO}/blob/main/whitepaper/AXCESS-WHITE-PAPER.md`;

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "beginning", text: "It started with one hard problem" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "growth", text: "Finding a problem is only the first question" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "principles", text: "What the project holds itself to" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "direction", text: "Where Axcess is going" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "open-source", text: "Built in the open at the University of Michigan" },
];

const direction = [
  {
    h: "Show exactly what was checked",
    p: 'Record what happened for every page, page state and check. "Found nothing", "skipped" and "not available" must never look the same.',
  },
  {
    h: "Test what people do, not just addresses",
    p: "Let a person record the steps of a task, with their permission, so Axcess can check the page after a click, a form error or a sign-in too.",
  },
  {
    h: "Treat issues as lasting work",
    p: "Keep what a check saw, what people decided, what was fixed and what a comparison showed as separate records.",
  },
  {
    h: "Make the report the product",
    p: "A complete, accessible web page (HTML) report that works without Axcess running. It says what was checked, what was not, and what to fix first.",
  },
  {
    h: 'Define "fully offline"',
    p: "A private live scan that contacts only the site being checked, and a replay mode that contacts nothing at all.",
  },
  {
    h: "Test new checks before trusting them",
    p: "Measure every new check against real examples that experts have reviewed before it is trusted.",
  },
];
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "About Axcess",
    description:
      "Why Axcess exists, how it grew from one problem into a tool for collecting evidence, and where it is going.",
  }}
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    Axcess started with one hard problem and grew into a way of working. This is the story, the idea that guides it, and
    where it is going.
  </p>

  <h2 id="beginning">It started with one hard problem</h2>
  <p>
    An accessibility lead at the University of Michigan needed to find every image that was really a picture of text,
    across a whole website, before the next report was due. Text inside an image can't be resized, restyled or read
    aloud. Existing tools could not find it well enough across a whole site, and checking every page and image by hand
    was slow, repetitive and easy to get wrong.
  </p>
  <p>The first version of Axcess did that one job. It:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>visited a website without sending anything to a cloud service</li>
    <li>found the images and read the text inside them</li>
    <li>compared that text with the image's alt text (the text a screen reader reads for an image)</li>
    <li>ranked the likely problems for a person to review</li>
    <li>scanned again to confirm the fixes</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The goal was never to replace the expert. It was to take away the repetitive search and give the expert better evidence.</p>

  <h2 id="growth">Finding a problem is only the first question</h2>
  <p>
    A raw result doesn't answer what people really ask. What happened? Who is affected? Which pages? How certain is it?
    What should change? How will we know it is fixed?
  </p>
  <p>
    So Axcess grew into a place to collect and review evidence. New checks came for page structure, names and roles,
    keyboard use, focus, small screens, media and meaning. A way to record manual testing came too, because software
    can't honestly answer many accessibility questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Axcess also became clear about how certain each result is. A failed rule, a behavior seen in the browser and an AI
    suggestion are never treated as equally certain. Each result keeps the name of the check that found it.
  </p>
  <p>The guiding idea is simple: <strong>keep the evidence before giving a verdict.</strong></p>

  <h2 id="principles">What the project holds itself to</h2>
  <!-- Two columns of cards at wide widths, one at narrow widths
       (SC 1.4.10 Reflow, Level AA). -->
  <div class="not-content cards two">
    <Card heading="Honest about what it checks">
      <p>
        Axcess's checks help with {covered} of the {total} Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 Level A and AA
        criteria, and it says so. Every number on this site comes from the same file the app reads, so a claim can't
        drift away from the code.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <!-- Changed from the old page's "No telemetry, no cloud AI, no
         account": adds the desktop app's update check
         (desktop/src/updates.cjs), which can be turned off. -->
    <Card heading="Private by default">
      <p>
        Evidence stays on the computer that ran the scan. There is no usage tracking (telemetry), no cloud AI and no
        account. The desktop app asks GitHub for updates when it opens, with no scan data, and you can turn that off.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <!-- docs/internal/design-principles.md: "When they pull apart,
         Universal Design wins". The app meets AAA where it can (AGENTS.md:
         44 px targets, 7:1 contrast, plain language), so "aims for". -->
    <Card heading="Universal design first">
      <p>
        The app aims for WCAG 2.2 Level AAA. It is designed around an accessibility professional who may use a screen
        reader, magnification or only a keyboard. When speed and universal design pull apart, universal design wins.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card heading="People decide, and the decisions are kept">
      <p>
        Automated results are a starting point, not the answer. People confirm, reject and fix them, with reasons and
        history, and the report says what was and wasn't checked.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </div>

  <h2 id="direction">Where Axcess is going</h2>
  <p>
    The project's white paper sets out its long-term direction: a private, offline tool built on evidence. It follows
    the World Wide Web Consortium's (W3C) method for evaluating websites (WCAG-EM) and the format for accessibility test rules (ACT rules). These
    are plans, not features Axcess has today.
  </p>
  <div class="not-content cards three">
    {
      direction.map((d) => (
        <Card heading={d.h}>
          <p>{d.p}</p>
        </Card>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <div class="not-content actions">
    <LinkButton href={WHITEPAPER} variant="secondary">Read the white paper</LinkButton>
  </div>

  <h2 id="open-source">Built in the open at the University of Michigan</h2>
  <p>
    Axcess is MIT licensed and developed by the LSA Technology Services team at the University of Michigan's College of
    Literature, Science, and the Arts.
    The code that visits pages, stores the evidence, shows the report and makes the exports is all public.
  </p>
  <!-- One filled button for the main action here (the source), the rest
       outlined, as on the home page: one main action per section. -->
  <div class="not-content actions">
    <LinkButton href={REPO}>Source code on GitHub</LinkButton>
    <LinkButton href={url("/docs/")} variant="secondary">Documentation</LinkButton>
    <LinkButton href={url("/get-started/")} variant="secondary">Get started</LinkButton>
  </div>
</StarlightPage>

<style>
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
  .two {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
  }
  .three {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 14rem), 1fr));
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
